# Velunara Full PRD

Velunara evaluates XPath expressions over XML trees and provides the associated node, datatype, regular-expression, schema integration, and result serialization capabilities.

### FP-01: XPath node trees

- Given an XML document, an XML element, or an existing XPath tree, callers obtain a tree usable for XPath evaluation. Documents and elements from Python's standard XML tree facility and compatible third-party XML trees are accepted. Other inputs, including a comment or processing instruction used as a root, are refused as invalid tree inputs.

- The tree exposes document, element, attribute, namespace, text, comment, and processing-instruction nodes when those kinds are present in the supplied data. Node kinds, expanded names, string values, parent and child relationships, node identity, and relative document order follow the node model of XQuery and XPath Data Model 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017, for the supplied source tree. Tree nodes representing elements, comments, processing instructions, or source documents carry the original XML objects; selecting those nodes returns the same source objects. Aggregate element and document string values are required for sources without text following retained comments or processing instructions.

- Namespace nodes use the source tree's namespace declarations when the source retains them. For a standard XML tree that does not retain declarations, caller-supplied namespace bindings provide this information. The XML namespace is available without an explicit binding. For document-root inputs, a supplied base URI is observable through the tree's URI accessors. For either document or element inputs, inherited XML base declarations participate in base-URI resolution according to XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017.

- By default, a document input retains a document root and an element input retains an element root. A third-party element with document-level siblings instead includes those siblings under a document root. Explicit fragment treatment selects the document element as the root and excludes document-level siblings. Explicit document treatment gives an element a document root. Under default element-root evaluation, selecting the document root returns no source document.

### FP-02: XPath and XML Schema values

- Callers can create, validate, and convert string, boolean, decimal, single- and double-precision floating-point, integer, nonpositive integer, negative integer, nonnegative integer, positive integer, signed 8-, 16-, 32-, and 64-bit integer, and unsigned 8-, 16-, 32-, and 64-bit integer values. The lexical rules and integer ranges are those of XML Schema Part 2: Datatypes Second Edition, W3C Recommendation of 28 October 2004, or W3C XML Schema Definition Language 1.1 Part 2: Datatypes, W3C Recommendation of 5 April 2012, according to the selected schema datatype version. Restricted integer ranges are enforced during direct construction and expression-based construction; separate validation checks lexical forms and constructed instances without a range guarantee. For single-precision values, separate validation covers constructed values and invalid lexical forms; acceptance of valid numeric lexical forms is required through construction. Numeric conversions, arithmetic, and ordering follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017, for the supplied operands and requested types. Integer arithmetic is exact; decimal arithmetic preserves exact results with at most eighteen significant decimal digits, and recurring decimal results are accurate to at least eighteen significant digits. Modulus with a negative divisor, modulus with a negative dividend of values constructed or converted as single- or double-precision numbers, and integer division of non-decimal operands whose exact quotient is a negative integer are outside this capability; modulus of integer or decimal operands with a negative dividend and a positive divisor remains required. Rejection of digit-group underscores inside numeric lexical forms is outside this capability. The default XML Schema datatype version for XPath evaluation is 1.0; a supplied schema's version takes precedence over an independently selected datatype version.

- The same construction and validation capability covers normalized strings, whitespace-collapsed tokens, language tags, XML names, names without colons, name tokens, identifiers, identifier references, entities, lists of name tokens, lists of identifier references, lists of entities, namespace-qualified names, notations, base64 binary values, hexadecimal binary values, and URI values. Their value and lexical rules follow the two XML Schema datatype Recommendations named above. Schema-derived notation construction supports conversion and enumeration constraints; retention of namespace identity and qualified-name operations on its result are outside this capability. A prefixed qualified name without a namespace URI is refused as an invalid value. Binary values can be decoded and re-encoded without changing their bytes. URI values refuse malformed percent escapes and more than one fragment delimiter as invalid values.

- Calendar values comprise dates, times, date-times, date-times requiring a timezone, Gregorian year, year-and-month, month, month-and-day, and day values, with timezone offsets. Duration values comprise general durations, durations measured in years and months, and durations measured in days and time. Their lexical forms, calendar rules, comparisons, and permitted arithmetic follow the selected XML Schema datatype Recommendation and XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017, using the supplied operands. Calendar arithmetic uses the implicit timezone where needed. Addition written with a day-and-time duration as the first operand and a date-time as the second is outside this capability; the calendar-value-first form remains required. Calendar comparison is required when both operands specify timezones or both omit them; comparison with only one timezone specified is outside this capability. Calendar and clock values preserve microsecond precision. Calendar years from 2147483648 BCE through 2147483648 CE are accepted where valid for the requested datatype; greater magnitudes are refused as overflow. Duration magnitudes may not exceed 2147483648 months or 9223372036854775808 seconds; exceeding either is refused as overflow. Invalid calendar fields, incompatible duration component signs, and offsets beyond fourteen hours in either direction are refused as invalid values.

- Untyped atomic values retain their lexical content and participate in conversions, comparisons, and arithmetic under the untyped-atomic rules of XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017, with the other operand determining the required conversion. Operations that require numbers refuse nonnumeric lexical content. Invalid lexical forms are refused as invalid values; construction also refuses out-of-range restricted integers as invalid values; incompatible operands are refused as type errors. Arithmetic failures are distinguished from lexical failures.

- For floating-point calculations with finite operands and results, whose nonzero operands and results are normal values for the requested precision and, for single precision, have magnitude at least ten to the negative thirty-seventh power, numerical results agree with the specified mathematical operation to within one part in a million of the magnitude of the expected nonzero result; an expected zero result is numerically zero. Comparisons of distinct single-precision values are required when each operand is zero or has magnitude at least ten to the negative thirty-seventh power, and the operands are separated by more than one part in a million of the larger operand magnitude. Single-precision behavior below that nonzero magnitude and distinctions between closer single-precision values are outside this capability.

### FP-03: Regular expressions and Unicode

- Callers can translate XML Schema and XPath regular expressions into patterns usable by the host regular-expression facility. Matching with the translated pattern must preserve the source pattern's meaning for the same input text and flags. XML Schema pattern rules follow the selected XML Schema Datatypes Recommendation, version 1.0 Second Edition of 28 October 2004 or version 1.1 of 5 April 2012. The default translation uses schema version 1.0 with the XPath extensions for capture groups, back references, lazy repetition, and anchors enabled; callers can disable those extensions to obtain whole-string XML Schema matching. Additional accepted syntax is outside the required pattern semantics; syntax beyond these Recommendations is not categorically required to be rejected.

- XPath matching, replacement, and tokenization follow XQuery 1.0 and XPath 2.0 Functions and Operators Second Edition, W3C Recommendation of 14 December 2010, and the version 3.0 extensions in XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014. Inputs are the text, pattern, selected XPath version, flags, and, for replacement, replacement text. Availability of literal interpretation in earlier versions is outside this capability. Malformed supported patterns, invalid flags other than the earlier-version literal flag, invalid replacement references, and zero-length matches where replacement or tokenization forbids them produce distinguishable regular-expression failures. XPath 3.0 also supports match analysis under that Recommendation.

- Character ranges, character-class subtraction, XML name-character escapes, Unicode general categories, and Unicode blocks participate in pattern matching according to the selected XML Schema pattern rules and the active Unicode database. Callers can query the active Unicode version, switch the supplied Unicode data among versions 13.0.0, 14.0.0, 15.0.0, 15.1.0, and 16.0.0, and restore the default, which is the Unicode database version of the Python runtime. Category and block membership after a switch follows the Unicode Character Database for the selected version, rather than the previously active version.

- Callers can supply Unicode category data for an explicitly selected version and use it for subsequent pattern translation. Accepted explicit versions are 2.0.0, 2.1.2, 2.1.5, 2.1.8, 2.1.9, 3.0.0, 3.0.1, 3.1.0, 3.1.1, 3.2.0, 4.0.0, 4.0.1, 4.1.0, 5.0.0, 5.1.0, 5.2.0, 6.0.0, 6.1.0, 6.2.0, 6.3.0, 7.0.0, 8.0.0, 9.0.0, 10.0.0, 11.0.0, 12.0.0, 12.1.0, 13.0.0, 14.0.0, 15.0.0, 15.1.0, and 16.0.0. An unrecognized version, or supplied data without an explicit version, is refused as invalid configuration. Versions before 13.0.0 require supplied category data for version-specific category results.

### FP-04: Evaluation context and reusable selection

- Given an expression and an XML tree, callers can request its complete result, consume its results one item at a time, or prepare the expression for repeated use with different trees and variable values. Repeated use applies the currently supplied data and values, without retaining the prior evaluation's selected nodes or variable values. An empty selection contains no result items.

- Callers can choose XPath 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, or 3.1 evaluation. XPath 2.0 is the default. An expression can also be evaluated without an XML root when a context item is supplied, or without a dynamic context for context-independent operations other than iteration and quantified expressions. A request to create a dynamic context with neither root nor item is refused as invalid context input. Without a dynamic context, expressions requiring focus or tree information are refused as missing-context operations.

- The initial context item is the supplied root unless the caller explicitly chooses a different item. Choosing a descendant as context item preserves access to the supplied root tree; supplying that descendant as the root instead restricts navigation to its subtree. Callers may set the context position and sequence size, which both default to one. Relative evaluation, position-dependent expressions, and sequence-size expressions use these supplied values according to the selected XPath Recommendation: XPath 1.0 of 16 November 1999, XPath 2.0 Second Edition of 14 December 2010, XPath 3.0 of 8 April 2014, or XPath 3.1 of 21 March 2017.

- Callers supply namespace bindings, variable bindings, an optional static base URI, an optional current date-time, and an optional implicit timezone. Namespace-qualified references and relative URI resolution use those bindings and base URI. A supplied current date-time is shared by the date, time, and date-time queries in that context; otherwise those queries use the host's current local date-time. A supplied implicit timezone governs timezone-dependent calendar arithmetic. On a host configured for UTC, the omitted implicit timezone is UTC; timezone-dependent evaluation on other hosts is required with an explicit implicit timezone. Variable values may be individual XPath items or sequences of them; an unbound referenced variable produces a missing-variable failure.

- In XPath 2.0 and later, callers may declare sequence types for variables, documents, named collections, and the default collection. Invalid sequence-type declarations are refused as invalid configuration. Bound values that do not match a declared type or cardinality are refused as type mismatches. Without declarations, variables allow arbitrary item sequences, documents require a document node, and collections allow any number of nodes. Name resolution and sequence-type matching follow the selected XPath Recommendation named above.

- Malformed expressions, unknown axes or functions, and incorrect function argument counts are refused as expression errors. Unbound namespace prefixes produce namespace-resolution failures. Operations on incompatible values produce type failures. Complete static-typing inference is outside this capability.

### FP-05: XPath 1.0 and 2.0 expressions

- XPath 1.0 navigation, expression grammar, operator precedence, conversions, and core functions follow XML Path Language 1.0, W3C Recommendation of 16 November 1999, for the expression, source nodes, namespace bindings, variable values, and context focus. Navigation covers the self, child, parent, descendant, descendant-or-self, ancestor, ancestor-or-self, following, preceding, following-sibling, preceding-sibling, attribute, and namespace axes; node-kind and name tests, namespace-qualified and wildcard names, abbreviated paths, predicates, and unions are supported. Path results use document order; for an abbreviated descendant step applied to more than one context node, the order of the combined result is outside this capability. String conversion of numbers is required for zero and for magnitudes from 0.0001 up to but excluding 10 to the 15th power; other magnitudes and negative zero are outside this capability. Equality involving node sets is required for string comparisons; implicit numeric or boolean conversion in node-set equality is outside this capability.

- XPath 1.0 core functions cover context position and size; node counting, identifier lookup, names, local names, and namespace URIs; string conversion, concatenation, prefix and substring checks, substring extraction, length, whitespace normalization, and character translation; boolean conversion, negation, boolean constants, and language testing; number conversion, sum, floor, ceiling, and rounding. The language also supports arithmetic, boolean operators, and comparisons under the same Recommendation. XPath 1.0 identifier lookup accepts a single XML identifier value and searches the supplied current tree using XML identifier attributes. Whitespace-separated identifier lists and identifiers available only through DTD declarations are outside this capability.

- XPath 2.0 grammar and expression semantics follow XML Path Language 2.0 Second Edition, W3C Recommendation of 14 December 2010, using the supplied static and dynamic context. This includes nested comments, sequence construction, empty sequences, ranges, iteration, quantified conditions, conditional branches, value and general comparisons, node identity and ordering comparisons, intersection and difference, integer division, kind and sequence-type tests, type assertions, and casts and castability checks. Where an iteration or quantified expression has more than one binding, a later binding expression given as a relative path is outside this capability; likewise, the cardinality check of an instance-of test whose operand is a sequence constructor of relative paths is outside this capability. Iteration and quantified expressions require a supplied dynamic context even for constant operands; without it they produce missing-context failures.

- In XPath 2.0 and later, the standard function families for numeric operations; string manipulation and Unicode code-point conversion; URI escaping and resolution; boolean operations; sequence existence, cardinality, indexing, insertion, removal, reversal, subsequences, distinctness, and aggregation; qualified-name construction and resolution; node access, atomization, root selection, deep equality, identifier and identifier-reference lookup; duration and calendar component extraction, timezone adjustment, and context date-time queries follow XQuery 1.0 and XPath 2.0 Functions and Operators Second Edition, W3C Recommendation of 14 December 2010, for the supplied arguments and context. Deep equality of nodes is required for node arguments selected by absolute paths or variables; node arguments given as relative paths are outside this capability. Resolution of a reference beginning with a single slash against a base whose path is not empty is outside this capability. Identifier-reference lookup matches whitespace-separated lexical tokens in element text or non-XML-ID attribute values, without requiring schema identifier-reference annotations. For standard-library XML trees, namespace URI lookup by prefix is required for bindings whose URI is used in element names within the queried subtree. String minimum and maximum aggregation is required with Unicode code-point ordering only. Unicode normalization covers canonical composition, canonical decomposition, compatibility composition, and compatibility decomposition; fully normalized Unicode checking is outside this capability.

- XPath 1.0 does not apply a default element namespace to unprefixed element names. XPath 2.0 and later use the caller's default element namespace, or no namespace when none is supplied; unprefixed attributes remain in no namespace. The default function namespace is the XPath functions namespace. XPath 1.0 compatibility conversions are enabled in XPath 1.0 and disabled by default in later versions, where callers may enable them; in those later versions, enabled compatibility conversions are required for arithmetic and for converting node operands to their first item, while general comparison between a number and a string is outside this capability. XPath 1.0 and 2.0 use standard name syntax by default and additionally accept expanded namespace names when the caller disables strict name syntax; XPath 3.0 and 3.1 use their standard URI-qualified name syntax.

- String comparisons can use Unicode code-point collation, ASCII case-insensitive collation for ASCII text, or a caller-selected installed host locale. An explicitly supplied default collation overrides the host-derived default. With no explicit collation, a configured UTF-8 collation locale supplies the default; otherwise Unicode code-point collation is used. The Unicode code-point and ASCII case-insensitive rules are those of XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017. Unsupported explicit collations produce collation failures. Full Unicode Collation Algorithm conformance is outside this capability.

- Explicit error signalling in an XPath 2.0 or later expression produces a failure with the supplied qualified error identity and description, or the standard default error identity when none is supplied, under XQuery 1.0 and XPath 2.0 Functions and Operators Second Edition, W3C Recommendation of 14 December 2010. A diagnostic trace expression returns its supplied item sequence unchanged.

### FP-06: Schema-aware evaluation

- Given a schema processor's declarations, type-conversion support, and an XML instance, XPath 2.0 and later can evaluate with schema-derived element and attribute types. Atomized values and type tests reflect those annotations instead of treating all content as untyped text. Schema element and attribute tests, custom atomic type constructors, casts, and instance tests follow the selected XPath Recommendation, version 2.0 Second Edition of 14 December 2010, 3.0 of 8 April 2014, or 3.1 of 21 March 2017, with the supplied schema and instance as inputs. Schema element tests are required where a matching child is present, and schema attribute tests where the tested attribute is the only attribute of its element; schema-typed boolean content other than the lexical true is outside this capability. Schema element and attribute tests cover a single matching declared node; expansion of substitution groups and general complex-type derivation membership through element kind tests are outside this capability. Retention of the integer subtype when atomizing a user restriction of integer is outside this capability; decimal-based custom types and directly declared built-in typed values remain supported.

- The integration permits callers to retrieve global type, element, and attribute declarations by qualified name, distinguish a missing declaration from a present declaration, test a value against a schema type, and convert a value to a schema-defined atomic type. A conversion that violates that type's constraints fails as an invalid value or type mismatch. An unknown requested type is distinguishable from an invalid value for an existing type. A value that is not a schema interface is refused as an invalid schema input.

- Schema component trees can serve as XPath roots for declaration selection. Selected declarations retain their source-object identity. For valid schemas and instances, substitution members and explicitly derived instance types retain their type annotations and typed content. Schema defaults and fixed values supply typed values, and schema-declared identifiers support identifier lookup while identifier-reference declarations supply typed reference values. Schema-sensitive identifier-reference selection is outside this capability. These annotation, value and lookup rules follow the supplied XML Schema 1.0 Second Edition of 28 October 2004 or XML Schema 1.1 of 5 April 2012.

### FP-07: Caller-defined XPath functions

- In XPath 2.0 and later, callers can register a host-language callable under an XPath function name and optional namespace, then invoke it from expressions. An omitted registration name uses the callable's host-language name; an omitted namespace uses the XPath functions namespace. Its returned value becomes the expression result and can participate in surrounding expressions. Registering it for one expression environment does not make it available in another independently created environment. The same local name may coexist in different namespaces.

- Required positional arguments and positional arguments with defaults determine the allowed argument counts; a callable with required positional arguments and additional variadic arguments accepts every count at or above its required count. Incorrect counts are refused as function-argument errors. Explicit sequence types constrain arguments and return values under XPath 2.0 Second Edition, W3C Recommendation of 14 December 2010; a mismatch fails as a type error. Arguments declared to accept multiple items receive the complete sequence.

- A registration name must be a valid XML name without colons under Namespaces in XML 1.0 Third Edition, W3C Recommendation of 8 December 2009, and must not be a reserved function name of the selected XPath version. A namespace prefix used for registration must be bound. Invalid names, missing prefix bindings, collisions with language constructs, and duplicate registration of a function in the same namespace are refused as registration failures.

### FP-08: XPath 3.0 expressions and functions

- XPath 3.0 expression extensions follow XML Path Language 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014, for the supplied expression and static and dynamic context. They cover local variable bindings, simple mapping, string concatenation, URI-qualified names, namespace-node tests, function-item tests, named function references, inline functions, dynamic function calls, and partial application. Function items can be selected by name and arity, inspected for their name and arity, and applied to arguments; name inspection of a function item obtained by dynamic lookup is outside this capability, while its arity and application remain required.

- Higher-order sequence mapping, filtering, left and right folds, and pairwise mapping follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014, with the supplied sequence order, function, and initial fold value. The same Recommendation governs taking the head and tail of a sequence, testing for children, selecting innermost and outermost nodes, and producing an absolute XPath for a node. Generated node identifiers are stable for repeated requests on the same node during an evaluation and differ for distinct nodes.

- Mathematical functions provide pi, exponential and base-ten exponential, natural and base-ten logarithms, power, square root, sine, cosine, tangent, inverse sine, inverse cosine, inverse tangent, and two-argument inverse tangent. Their arguments, results, empty-sequence behavior, and nonfinite cases follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014. Natural and base-ten logarithms of arguments strictly between minus one and zero are outside this capability. Raising a negative base to a fractional decimal exponent is outside this capability; the corresponding double-precision operation remains supported.

- Given a value, a formatting picture, and optional language and decimal-format settings, integer, number, date, time, and date-time formatting follow the picture rules of XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014. Integer presentation covers decimal digits, grouping and padding, Latin alphabetic numbering, Roman numerals, and English or Italian words, with English as the default. Calendar formatting covers numeric fields and English or Italian names in the Gregorian calendar. A named decimal format uses the caller's supplied symbols; omitted symbols use that Recommendation's default decimal-format symbols. Pictures with invalid grammar, calendar fields absent from the input value, or a requested named decimal format that has not been declared produce formatting failures. Refusal of a number-formatting picture in which an optional digit follows a mandatory digit in the integer part is outside this capability. Localization beyond these languages and non-Gregorian calendar conversion are outside this capability.

- String joining supports an omitted separator. Numeric rounding accepts a caller-selected decimal precision. Their results follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014, for the supplied sequence or number, separator, and precision. Negative-precision rounding exactly halfway between two candidate results is outside this capability.

### FP-09: Documents, text resources, XML parsing, and environment access

- In XPath 2.0 and later, document and collection queries resolve relative references using the supplied static base URI and retrieve the caller's URI-associated documents or ordered node collections. A query without a collection reference uses the caller's default collection; an absent default collection fails as an unavailable resource. Missing documents or collections fail as unavailable resources; document availability queries return false for missing documents. Retrieval follows XQuery 1.0 and XPath 2.0 Functions and Operators Second Edition, W3C Recommendation of 14 December 2010, using the supplied URI bindings. Document queries do not download an unregistered document, and collections are not discovered by scanning a directory. An unbound collection reference identifying an existing local directory is refused as an invalid collection reference.

- XPath 3.0 and later can read text from local files or a locally hosted HTTP or FTP service, return the whole text or its lines, and check resource availability. The input reference, optional encoding, static base URI, and supplied text-resource bindings determine the result under XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014. The default encoding is UTF-8; UTF-16 text identified by its byte-order mark is also readable without an explicit encoding. A supplied text binding takes precedence over opening the same URI. URI collection queries return caller-supplied ordered URI collections; a query without a reference returns the caller's default resource URI as a singleton and fails as an unavailable resource if that default is absent. An unconfigured named URI collection returns no items when no URI collections have been supplied, and fails as unavailable when other URI collections have been supplied.

- Opening external text and JSON resources is disabled by default. Callers may enable access or restrict network resources to a set of allowed base-URL prefixes. For local files in ordinary users' home directories or temporary storage, callers can likewise restrict opening to permitted base-location prefixes. The restriction compares the requested URL after normalizing path dot segments, and a request outside every permitted prefix is refused as a resource-access failure. Availability queries return false for denied or unavailable text resources. Text retrieval refuses references with fragments, unknown encodings, undecodable text, and characters outside XML's character repertoire as URI, encoding, or text failures. An explicitly supplied text binding can be read while external opening is disabled; availability queries still obey the opening permission.

- Given XML 1.0 text and a dynamic context, XPath 3.0 and later can parse a complete document or a fragment containing text and multiple top-level elements into XPath nodes, under XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.0, W3C Recommendation of 8 April 2014. Malformed XML is refused as an XML parsing failure. Entity declarations are forbidden by default when parsing complete documents; callers can disable that protection for trusted documents. Fragment parsing with document type declarations or entity declarations is outside this capability.

- Reading host environment variables is disabled by default: individual reads and enumeration reveal no variables. Callers may enable all variables or allow selected names and name prefixes. An allowed existing variable returns its actual value; an absent or denied variable returns no value. Enumeration reveals only existing allowed names. Resource-access and environment-access policy configuration must be a boolean choice or a sequence of permission entries; other configuration types are refused as invalid configuration.

### FP-10: XPath 3.1 maps, arrays, and JSON

- XPath 3.1 map and array expressions, unary and postfix lookup, arrow application, and function application to an array of arguments follow XML Path Language 3.1 and XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendations of 21 March 2017, for the supplied expression, context, keys, values, members, and functions. Missing map entries yield an empty sequence; duplicate keys in a map constructor, invalid array positions, and incompatible lookup operands fail as duplicate-key, array-bounds, or type errors.

- The map operations are size, key enumeration, membership, retrieval, addition or replacement of an entry, entry removal, single-entry construction, merging, recursive finding, and applying a function to each entry. The array operations are size, retrieval, replacement, insertion, appending, removal, subarray extraction, head, tail, reversal, joining, flattening, elementwise and pairwise mapping, filtering, left and right folding, and sorting. Sequence sorting also accepts an optional collation and key function. Their outcomes follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017. Merge duplicates retain the first entry by default; callers can choose first, last, any one, rejection, or combination in encounter order. Combination of duplicate entries is required for singleton values.

- JSON text parsing and authorized JSON-resource reading convert JSON objects to maps, arrays to arrays, strings, booleans, and numbers to atomic values, and null to an empty sequence. They follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017, using the supplied text or resource contents and options. Duplicate object names retain the first value by default; callers can choose first, last, or rejection. Malformed JSON, an unknown duplicate policy, and incorrectly typed options fail as JSON syntax, option, or type errors. Strict JSON syntax is the default, apart from the non-JSON number tokens for not-a-number and infinity, whose rejection is outside this capability; callers can enable the relaxed handling of control characters within strings.

- JSON string escapes are decoded by default. A caller may instead retain escaped characters, or supply a single-argument character-replacement function for characters that XML cannot represent. In JSON text and resource parsing, without either choice, such characters are replaced by the Unicode replacement character. Default replacement of XML-illegal characters during JSON-to-XML conversion is outside this capability; caller-supplied character replacement remains supported. Simultaneous escaped-character retention and a character-replacement function are outside this capability. Conversion between JSON text and the standard XML representation of JSON follows XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017, preserving object names, member order within arrays, atomic content, and nulls for XML-representable strings. JSON-to-XML permits escaped-character retention and duplicate-name choices of retention, rejection, or keeping the first value. Without a duplicate choice, names are retained unless validation of the standard XML representation is requested, in which case duplicates are rejected. Validation is disabled by default; combining validation with explicit duplicate retention is refused as invalid options. Conversion of object names containing double quotation marks and nondefault XML-to-JSON formatting options are outside this capability.

- Date parsing for Internet date strings, except numeric timezone offsets written with a single hour digit, and whitespace-token containment follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017, for the supplied string and collation. Collation-key generation returns binary keys whose equality agrees with equality of the corresponding strings under the selected collation. Random generation returns a number from zero inclusive to one exclusive and permits continued generation. Within an uninterrupted continuation, numbers behave as independent uniform samples over that interval. A supplied finite sequence can be permuted while preserving all members and multiplicities; within an uninterrupted continuation, permutations of a fixed finite sequence behave as independent uniform permutations of its positions. Repeating generation with the same explicit seed reproduces the initial number.

- XPath 3.1 string joining converts supplied atomic items to strings before joining them, and the generic numeric conversion preserves numeric input or converts lexical numeric input to a numeric value. These operations follow XPath and XQuery Functions and Operators 3.1, W3C Recommendation of 21 March 2017. A default-language query with an explicitly supplied context language returns that language; deriving a default language from an unconfigured host locale is outside this capability.

### FP-11: XPath result serialization

- Given XPath values and a dynamic context, callers can serialize results to XML, or, in XPath 3.1 with map-based serialization options, to JSON. XML is the default method. Missing dynamic context produces a missing-context failure. XML serialization of document and element nodes preserves element names, namespace meaning, attributes, and descendant text and element order; the result can be parsed back to the same XML content. This preservation promise concerns elements without text following their closing tag in the source. Standalone attribute and namespace nodes and nonserializable function items are refused as serialization failures. HTML, XHTML, text, and adaptive output methods are outside this capability.

- XML serialization accepts an XML serialization-parameter element in XPath 3.0 and later, or a parameter map in XPath 3.1. Callers can choose whether to include an XML declaration, specify an item separator, and supply single-character replacements. The declaration is omitted by default. Consecutive atomic items are separated by one space unless an item separator is supplied; that separator also separates serialized node items. Documents contribute their children and arrays contribute their flattened members. Character replacement applies to the serialized result. Atomic XML text containing markup-significant characters and interactions among overlapping character replacements are outside this capability. Duplicate XML parameter entries, invalid values for declaration, separator and character-map options, and character-map keys that are not single characters are refused as serialization-parameter failures.

- JSON serialization preserves strings, booleans, finite numbers, maps with string keys, and arrays as their corresponding JSON values. An empty input sequence becomes JSON null. Empty sequences stored as array members become JSON null. An empty sequence stored as a map value retains its member name and becomes an empty JSON array. More than one top-level item is refused as a serialization-cardinality failure. Nonfinite numbers and nonserializable function items are refused as serialization failures. Top-level XML nodes serialize as JSON strings containing their XML representation; nodes stored as array members or map values are outside this capability. Decimal values requiring more than two fractional decimal digits, maps whose keys collide only after conversion to JSON names, and map entries or array members containing more than one item are outside this capability; integral and floating-point numbers and singleton or empty members remain supported.
